<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>1.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>1</string>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UILaunchScreen</key>
	<dict/>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
	</array>

	<!-- Nova is on the household LAN, so every connection this app makes is a
	     local-network connection. Without this key iOS refuses them outright
	     and reports NSURLErrorNotConnectedToInternet (-1009), which reads as
	     "no internet" and sends you looking at the wrong layer entirely. -->
	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Nova Companion connects to your Nova voice server on the local network to answer requests on this device.</string>

	<!-- Calendar and Reminders are read only when a request needs them, in
	     bounded windows, and the scoped results may be sent to your own Nova
	     server to answer that request. Nothing is written without an explicit
	     approval on this device. These strings are shown verbatim in the iOS
	     prompt, so they say what actually happens rather than a generic
	     "to provide features". -->
	<key>NSCalendarsFullAccessUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Nova reads a bounded range of your calendar when you ask it something that needs one, and only changes an event after you approve the exact change on this phone.</string>
	<key>NSRemindersFullAccessUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Nova reads your reminders when you ask it something that needs them, and only adds, completes or removes one after you approve the exact change on this phone.</string>

	<!-- "When in use" only. The app answers location questions while it is
	     running; asking for Always would request a capability it does not need.
	     Your coordinates never leave the phone — Nova is told only whether you
	     are near home, which is the question it actually asks. -->
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Nova checks whether this phone is near home so it can tell whether you are in. Your location stays on the phone; Nova is told only yes or no.</string>

	<!-- The voice server presents a household CA certificate, not a public one,
	     which App Transport Security rejects by default. NSAllowsLocalNetworking
	     relaxes ATS for local addresses only — public hosts keep full ATS, so
	     this does not weaken anything reachable from outside the house. Server
	     identity is still verified: the client presents its own certificate and
	     signs a challenge, which is a stronger check than ATS performs.

	     NSAllowsLocalNetworking does not cover the tailnet. Tailscale addresses
	     are in 100.64.0.0/10 (RFC 6598 shared address space), not the RFC 1918
	     ranges ATS treats as local, so away-from-home connections got full ATS
	     and failed with -1200 while the identical LAN address was fine. The
	     exception below is by tailnet domain rather than address because ATS
	     exception keys are domain names and silently do not match IPs — which
	     is also why the endpoint is now the MagicDNS name.

	     NSExceptionAllowsInsecureHTTPLoads reads worse than it is: it removes
	     ATS's requirement for a *publicly* trusted chain on this one domain.
	     What replaces it is stricter, not weaker — the app anchors the
	     household CA alone, with system roots switched off, so a public
	     certificate for the same name is now refused rather than accepted. -->
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
		<true/>
		<key>NSExceptionDomains</key>
		<dict>
			<key>tuatara-dory.ts.net</key>
			<dict>
				<key>NSIncludesSubdomains</key>
				<true/>
				<key>NSExceptionAllowsInsecureHTTPLoads</key>
				<true/>
				<key>NSExceptionMinimumTLSVersion</key>
				<string>TLSv1.2</string>
			</dict>
		</dict>
	</dict>
</dict>
</plist>
